WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
henry morgan's daring borderline reckless&nbsp;
raids became the stuff of legend and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.200
successfully plundered more than his fair&nbsp;
share of booty in the west indies and best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.960
of all he got away with it henry morgan&nbsp;
did not die in battle and did not end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.400
on the end of a noose few other captains&nbsp;
from his time had that privilege and none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.240
of them did it quite like morgan who&nbsp;
after his plundering days were done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:24.080
became a nobleman and a politician reveling in the&nbsp;
admiration and respect of his peers so was he a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.360
pirate or was he a privateer the answer really&nbsp;
depends on who you ask but there is no denying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:43.840
that henry morgan has earned his spot as one of&nbsp;
the most notorious men to sail the seven seas

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.320
henry morgan's early life is very obscure although&nbsp;
most sources indicate that he was born circa 1635&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.640
in the welsh town of lien romney which today&nbsp;
is a sub of cardiff we know little else about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.120
his upbringing although a contemporary of the&nbsp;
pirate named alexandre x milor claimed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.840
morgan came from a wealthy farming family but&nbsp;
that he had no interest in that life which is why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.560
he became a sailor equivalent will serve as one of&nbsp;
our main sources for the life of henry morgan he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.200
quite distinctive in that regard not only was he a&nbsp;
contemporary of the welsh pirates but he actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.800
served on morgan's ship and was an eyewitness&nbsp;
to many of his exploits you'd think that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.400
would make him one of the most credible sources&nbsp;
we've ever had on biographics but he was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.800
sued for libel by henry morgan who claimed that he&nbsp;
made up or exaggerated certain stories in his book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.000
history of the buccaneers of america in order to&nbsp;
make morgan appear more murderous and cruel well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.080
if we're being technical morgan actually sued&nbsp;
the publisher of the english translation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.000
the book but the point is that he won however&nbsp;
the matter was settled out of court so whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.720
or not x-com allah's statements were true was&nbsp;
never established with certainty we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.280
assume that most of what ex-cubanol said about&nbsp;
henry morgan was true even if he did embellish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.840
certain parts in this case morgan left his old&nbsp;
life behind sometime during the mid-1650s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.000
traveled to an english port city from where&nbsp;
he boarded a ship to barbados in the west&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.320
indies when he arrived there he was sold as an&nbsp;
indentured servant for a period of three years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:10.320
this is just one version of events another claimed&nbsp;
that morgan simply joined a british navy ship to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.560
fight in the anglo-spanish war either way&nbsp;
he made his way to the west indies where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:21.120
the better known chapter of his life began in 1655&nbsp;
the english tried to take the island of hispaniola&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.000
from the spanish they failed but they did&nbsp;
manage to take jamaica with an assist from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.040
the buccaneers we should mention at this point&nbsp;
that although nowadays we use terms like pirate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.160
privateer and buccaneer interchangeably back&nbsp;
then they did mean slightly different things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:42.080
and it's worth knowing the difference since henry&nbsp;
morgan served as all three at various times we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.960
all familiar with pirates they had no loyalty to&nbsp;
any government or king they operated outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.920
the law and attacked whoever struck their fancy&nbsp;
privateers on the other hand were basically state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:57.120
licensed pirates they received something called&nbsp;
a letter of mark from the admiralty or a governor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.440
and this allowed them to raid certain ships which&nbsp;
usually belonged to another nation that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.160
currently at war with the privateer captain had&nbsp;
to secure his ship and crew out of his own pocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.960
and he had to send a share of the spoils to his&nbsp;
government but he was not breaking the law and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.440
he had no fear of legal reprisals in fact some&nbsp;
of history's most successful privateers such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.840
francis drake someone we've already covered here&nbsp;
on biographics you can check out the video if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.280
you haven't seen it were regarded as heroes back&nbsp;
home finally we have buccaneers who were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.600
a very specific subgroup of sailors who appeared&nbsp;
in the caribbean sea during the early 17th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.160
they consisted of a lot of seamen who were forced&nbsp;
to flee the pirate haven of tortuga when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:41.840
spanish took control of it in 1635. also included&nbsp;
were social outcasts such as shipwreck survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.800
mutineers and escaped slaves as well as native&nbsp;
americans who settled in parts of the west indies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.040
that the spanish didn't bother to colonize when&nbsp;
other european nations such as england france or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.920
the netherlands sailed in the caribbean they found&nbsp;
most spanish ports closed to them and that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.240
people were the only ones willing to trade the&nbsp;
english started calling them buccaneers from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.560
french word buccaneer itself derived from bukhar&nbsp;
which is a wooden grill used to smoke meat and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.400
make a jerky that they often used for trade&nbsp;
it wasn't until the late 17th century that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.640
buccaneers became powerful enough that they&nbsp;
earned the ire of other nations during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.680
1650s and 1660s however england was more than&nbsp;
happy to trade with buccaneers and allow them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:27.840
to use its port since they represented&nbsp;
a cheap way of waging war with spain

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.520
once henry morgan was settled in the west&nbsp;
indies around the mid-1650s he began his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.320
career as a privateer although the exact details&nbsp;
are a bit uncertain it's generally believed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.040
he sailed under christopher mungs an english naval&nbsp;
officer who assembled a large fleet of buccaneers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.440
and raided spanish merchant ships excumiore&nbsp;
mentions that morgan and his comrades took part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.240
in three or four such voyages before they raised&nbsp;
enough money to buy their own ship around 1660&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.720
henry morgan who was still in his mid-20s was&nbsp;
made captain of his first vessel he proved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.920
be a very good one too as he went marauding off&nbsp;
the coast of campeche in the gulf of mexico and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:09.520
captured several prizes there his success brought&nbsp;
him to the attention of edward mansfeld a dutch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.560
buccaneer who was one of the head honchos in the&nbsp;
area and he was impressed with morgan's skill so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.640
he asked the young captain to join his fleet even&nbsp;
making him a vice admiral at the height of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:24.560
power mansfield allegedly commanded 15 ships with&nbsp;
500 men in 1664 he used this force to capture an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:29.760
island named santa catalina from spain it's&nbsp;
not very big but it had an easily fortified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.080
harbor and the spanish had already built a fort&nbsp;
there mansfeld intended to use it as a base of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:40.000
operations as he waged war against spain but he&nbsp;
did not get a chance as he died in 1666 supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.720
of a sudden illness the spanish then retook santa&nbsp;
catalina and many buccaneers went their own ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.200
without mansfeld's leadership even so hundreds&nbsp;
of others still remained and sailed under henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:54.240
morgan the second in command who was now in charge&nbsp;
of his own fleet his actions at the time were in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.280
illegal gray areas since technically the war&nbsp;
between england and spain had ended in 1660 when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.160
the house of stewart was restored to the monarchy&nbsp;
and charles ii became the new king of england&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:09.920
scotland and ireland even so hostilities between&nbsp;
the two maritime powers continued in the caribbean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.480
enabled by the fact that privateers and buccaneers&nbsp;
could act without much oversight from the english&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:19.040
crown henry was also helped by the fact that&nbsp;
his uncle edward morgan served as the deputy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.760
governor of jamaica and by the fact that the&nbsp;
governor thomas martiford already tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.000
curtail the activity of privateers but almost&nbsp;
immediately had to reverse course because his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.440
island's economy just depended on them too much&nbsp;
of course spain wasn't just gonna sit idly by and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.280
allow its ships to be constantly raided without&nbsp;
retaliation by 1667 governor marty ford had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.600
strong reasons to fear that the spanish might&nbsp;
invade jamaica so he decided that a pre-emptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.000
strike would be in order he presented henry&nbsp;
morgan with a letter of mark against spain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.680
thus allowing him to attack in full force even&nbsp;
though they were not at war at the time almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:59.040
immediately morgan exceeded his authority by&nbsp;
raiding the town of puerto principa in cuba today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.800
known as camogue his letter of mark only allowed&nbsp;
him to attack spanish ships but towns made for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.880
very alluring targets since they were often easier&nbsp;
to plunder especially if caught off guard this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.680
the main reason why some scholars have dismissed&nbsp;
the notion that henry morgan was a privateer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:21.920
and simply label him a pirate since he often acted&nbsp;
outside of his remit initially the captain wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:26.960
to attack havana believing that a surprise attack&nbsp;
would allow them to arrive in the city plunder it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.600
and leave before the spanish had time to reach&nbsp;
their positions inside the fort however he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:36.400
dissuaded from this course of action on the&nbsp;
ground that havana was too heavily fortified and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.520
even a large fleet such as his would sustain&nbsp;
heavy casualties instead morgan settled on puerto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.320
principe as an excuse for his actions he sent&nbsp;
word to the governor that he found evidence of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:52.240
invasion against jamaica being organized there in&nbsp;
reality at least according to ectomy there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.880
man in morgan's crew who suggested puerto principe&nbsp;
claiming that there was money there since savannah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.280
merchants did a lot of business in that town and&nbsp;
because it was some distance inland so there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:05.680
no fear of raiders so it wasn't heavily defended&nbsp;
even though it made for an easy target the raid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:11.360
on puerto principe didn't go very well first off&nbsp;
there was a spanish prisoner aboard morgan's ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:15.360
who learned of their plan he managed to escape&nbsp;
during the night and reached puerto principe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.920
in time to warn the locals who then sent most of&nbsp;
their riches away and then chopped down trees and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.640
set up small ambushes on the road to slow down&nbsp;
the raiding party inevitably though the fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:28.320
started at which point the spanish villagers&nbsp;
proved to be no match for the battle hardened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:33.520
buccaneers the skirmishes lasted for about four&nbsp;
hours ending in a decisive victory for morgan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:37.840
crew who suffered only a few casualties afterwards&nbsp;
the locals were locked up in the churches while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.960
the pirates plundered their town at this point&nbsp;
according to exumior morgan starved and tortured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.520
the townsfolk in order to get them to surrender&nbsp;
their riches eventually he agreed to allow a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.360
of them to leave the town and travel to another&nbsp;
city to secure a ransom for the release of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.280
prisoners but he soon discovered that he had&nbsp;
been deceived instead of getting the money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.280
the town's people were delaying him as long&nbsp;
as possible as the governor of santiago was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.000
sending an army to puerto principe left no other&nbsp;
choice morgan carried what little plunder he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:09.280
aboard his ships and departed the town before the&nbsp;
reinforcements arrived and if that was not bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:13.520
enough he also had to contend with a possible riot&nbsp;
following an argument between an english and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:18.160
french privateer which resulted in the englishman&nbsp;
killing the frenchman to show that he did not play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:23.200
favorites morgan had the english sailor chained&nbsp;
up and delivered him to stand trial in jamaica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.520
where he was hanged even so the french were still&nbsp;
unhappy sailing under henry morgan's command and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:35.840
most of them around 200 men in all left his&nbsp;
fleet soon after and went back to tortuga

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.960
the less than stellar attack on puerto principe&nbsp;
did not diminish morgan's ambitions and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:48.160
chose as his next target the town of portobello&nbsp;
located in modern-day panama this was a high-risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:52.560
reward situation as the settlement was heavily&nbsp;
fortified but also sat on the principal trading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:58.640
route between the spanish maine and spain meaning&nbsp;
that it always had valuable cargo import in july&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:04.480
1668 morgan anchored his ships near portobello&nbsp;
again he preferred a stealthy approach and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.600
time there was nobody to spoil his surprise&nbsp;
attack his men boarded a few dozen canoes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.000
paddled at night in silence reaching portobello&nbsp;
and starting their raid before dawn they caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.320
the locals unaware and even though the town had&nbsp;
three castles few spanish soldiers actually had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:22.960
time to reach their fortified positions allegedly&nbsp;
morgan devised a particularly cruel strategy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:27.200
protect his men during their assault on the&nbsp;
last castle by using spanish priests and nuns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:31.680
as human shields this is all according to ex&nbsp;
camillon who claimed that morgan gathered the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:36.080
religious men and women of portobello and had&nbsp;
them place ladders on the walls of the castle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:40.800
and then climbed them ahead of his companies&nbsp;
so that the enemy would not open fire something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:45.440
which they did anyway this was the part in&nbsp;
excamino's book that henry morgan objected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:49.840
to and later successfully sued for libel he got&nbsp;
it removed from the english translations of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:53.760
book but since the matter was settled out of&nbsp;
court we still don't know for sure if morgan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:59.600
actually ever used nuns as human shields either&nbsp;
way his raid on portobello was a big success he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:03.920
not only plundered the town for many riches but&nbsp;
held it for ransom afterwards the spanish made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.840
an attempt to retake portobello by force&nbsp;
but the pirates repelled the attack and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:11.840
then the local governor had no choice but to&nbsp;
pay the ransom of a hundred thousand pesos or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:17.760
have the entire town reduced to ashes everyone on&nbsp;
morgan's crew left portobello a rich man receiving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:23.040
a share worth several times their annual wages of&nbsp;
course as captain henry morgan received a larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:28.560
share as did governor moddy ford for issuing the&nbsp;
letter of mark there were some legal ramifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.440
since as we previously mentioned england and spain&nbsp;
were not at war at the time ultimately though both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:37.920
morgan and the governor got off with slaps on&nbsp;
the wrist since realistically everyone back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.400
england still considered spain to be their enemy&nbsp;
and realized that a new war was just never too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:46.240
far away in the months that followed morgan was&nbsp;
already preparing for his next raid but he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:51.760
a very close call when one of his ships exploded&nbsp;
with him on board in 1668 he received some heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:55.920
reinforcements in the form of hms oxford or&nbsp;
warship that governor moriford obtained from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:00.400
the royal navy to help defend jamaica the governor&nbsp;
added its morgan's fleet who then promptly made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:05.840
it his new flagship however he did not get&nbsp;
to enjoy it for long because in january 1669&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:10.720
an errant spark from a pig roast went into the&nbsp;
oxford's powder magazine blowing up the ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:14.880
morgan was one of the lucky ones because he was&nbsp;
situated at the far end of the ship he was in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:19.360
cabin eating and drinking with the captains of his&nbsp;
fleet the explosion simply propelled him out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:25.360
the window and onto the water but 350 of his men&nbsp;
were not so fortunate and perished in the blast

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:36.720
clearly with almost half his crew dead morgan&nbsp;
needed to reassess the situation initially he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:41.840
wanted to attack cartagena to india's one of&nbsp;
the biggest port cities on the spanish main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:46.640
located today in colombia but now that was&nbsp;
off the table instead he decided to repeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:52.560
the rating route that french pirate francois&nbsp;
olyny did two years earlier in lake maracaibo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:58.880
a tidal bay in modern-day venezuela this involved&nbsp;
two main targets the city of maracaibo itself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:03.920
the town of gibraltar not to be confused with&nbsp;
gibraltar in the iberian peninsula first came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.880
maracaibo which did not prove to be difficult&nbsp;
since it had been mostly abandoned the privateers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.640
spent a few weeks in the city time during which&nbsp;
they took everything of value that was left behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:16.800
they also scoured the nearby jungles knowing&nbsp;
that some of the locals would not have traveled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:20.880
very far they were then brought back to maracaibo&nbsp;
where they were tortured to reveal the locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:25.360
where they hid their valuables afterwards morgan&nbsp;
sailed across lake maracaibo to gibraltar where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:30.720
he encountered heavier resistance but nothing that&nbsp;
posed a serious threat he spent a few more weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:34.880
there but when he wanted to leave he discovered&nbsp;
that he had been trapped three spanish warships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:41.520
under the command of don alonso del campo espanosa&nbsp;
waited for him at the mouth of the lake there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:47.280
no getting around them without a fight but what&nbsp;
morgan did next cemented his place in nautical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:51.440
history the spanish captain actually sent him&nbsp;
a letter offering him clemency provided that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:55.520
give up all of his plunder and prisoners morgan&nbsp;
brought this option to his men who immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.520 --> 00:14:01.440
turned it down preferring to fight and die then&nbsp;
give up their booty but morgan had a better idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:06.560
using subterfuge to defeat his enemy he sacrificed&nbsp;
one of his vessels and turned it into a fire ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:10.560
a ship stuffed with explosives that was&nbsp;
deliberately steered into the enemy formation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:14.960
and abandoned by its crew he also filled the&nbsp;
deck of the ship with giant wooden logs which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:21.040
were dressed in coats and hats to disguise them as&nbsp;
a regular crew the plan worked on april 30th 1669&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:25.360
the pirates launched their attack the spanish did&nbsp;
not spot the deception until it was too late and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:31.120
the fire ship plowed straight into espinosa's&nbsp;
flagship the magdalena which was destroyed but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:35.680
that was not the end as one of the remaining&nbsp;
two warships was run aground in the chaos while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:40.400
the other one was taken over by the buccaneers&nbsp;
and added to morgan's fleet and so henry morgan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:44.880
sailed out of lake maracaibo and into legend&nbsp;
as one of the most daring pirates in history

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:55.600
understandably the spanish were none too pleased&nbsp;
with morgan's actions so under orders from queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.600 --> 00:15:00.000
regent maria anna their privateers wrapped up&nbsp;
their raids on english merchant ships in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.120
region in response modefoot commissioned morgan to&nbsp;
commit his most ambitious attack yet an assault on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:10.000
the city of panama one of the main settlements on&nbsp;
the spanish mainland this was a multi-part plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:14.480
since panama wasn't the kind of place they could&nbsp;
just stroll into first morgan re took the island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:19.440
of santa catalina from the spanish he used it as&nbsp;
a headquarters where he gathered men and supplies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:23.680
and discussed the details of his scheme with&nbsp;
his captains estimates of the size of his army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:29.600
are all over the place but most agree that it was&nbsp;
around 1 800 to 2 000 men from santa catalina the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:35.040
pirates left for panama approaching by sailing up&nbsp;
the shangra river this also meant capturing fort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:39.440
san lorenzo which overlooked the mouth of the&nbsp;
river since morgan did not want to be trapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:43.600
again when it was time to leave this was easier&nbsp;
said than done there was the fort at a garrison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:49.200
of over 300 men and 20 cannons morgan lost around&nbsp;
100 men in the fight and 17 more were wounded but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:53.280
the tide turned in his favor when a musket shot&nbsp;
caused the powder magazine to blow up once the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:58.240
port was under his command morgan left around 500&nbsp;
men behind and took the remaining 1 200 through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:03.680
the panamanian jungle in january 1671. this trek&nbsp;
lasted for nine days the spanish knew of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:08.320
impending arrival and had employed scorched earth&nbsp;
tactics burning down the villages and the crops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:12.640
that stood between the pirates in panama when the&nbsp;
buccaneers arrived within sight of the city they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:18.240
were hungry tired and demoralized on top of this&nbsp;
the spanish greatly outnumbered them and they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:23.680
two squadrons of cavalry they were expecting an&nbsp;
easy victory against a ragtag band of pirates led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:28.800
by the son of a welsh farmer but they got exactly&nbsp;
the opposite the cavalry was taken care of when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:33.440
morgan fained a retreat by sending part of&nbsp;
his army to one flank tricking the horsemen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:37.920
into pursuing them straight into the line of fire&nbsp;
of his remaining forces once the cavalry was out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:42.720
of the way the buccaneers charged into the city&nbsp;
which had almost no fortification so the fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:47.920
was done in the open the spanish infantry although&nbsp;
greater in number proved no match for the pirates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:52.640
and it mainly consisted of locals and slaves&nbsp;
none of whom were experienced in battle morgan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:57.200
had taken panama which was pillaged and burned to&nbsp;
the ground over the following weeks it was never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:02.240
rebuilt and today the ruins of old panama are&nbsp;
a unesco world heritage site while the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:06.720
panama city was built a few miles to the west&nbsp;
but whether or not henry morgan was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:11.360
responsible for its obliteration is still a matter&nbsp;
of debate as some sources claimed that it was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:17.200
spanish general who ordered its destruction as he&nbsp;
would rather see it burn and fall into enemy hands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:21.760
the mission had been a success but the spoils&nbsp;
were surprisingly meager mainly this was because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:26.800
the plunder had to be shared among so many men&nbsp;
but excavator did accuse morgan of sneaking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:31.680
lion's share of the riches onto his ship either&nbsp;
way when the welsh captain arrived in jamaica he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:37.200
expected a hero's welcome but what he got instead&nbsp;
was an international incident in july of 1670&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:42.240
england and spain had signed the treaty of madrid&nbsp;
specifically intended to finally settle their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:47.120
disputes in america spain agreed to recognize&nbsp;
jamaica as belonging to england and in return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:51.600
england had to end all private curing against&nbsp;
spanish ships the fact that morgan burned to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:56.160
ground one of the biggest cities on the spanish&nbsp;
mains so soon after the treaty was signed what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.160 --> 00:18:01.520
would today call a diplomatic faux pas both morgan&nbsp;
and governor modified were arrested and sent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:06.800
london to stand trial in an attempt to appease&nbsp;
spain henry morgan would have been in serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:11.360
trouble if he was convicted of piracy fortunately&nbsp;
for him public opinion was firmly on his side as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:15.520
many considered him a hero and a skilled military&nbsp;
commander he also had a pretty solid defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:20.480
arguing that he did not know about the treaty when&nbsp;
he set off for panama whether or not this was true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:25.760
well who knows but it was good enough to get him&nbsp;
off the hook in fact king charles ii even knighted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:30.560
him making him a baronet in 1674 and appointing&nbsp;
him the new lieutenant governor of jamaica the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:35.360
now sir henry morgan returned to his old stomping&nbsp;
grounds in jamaica where he spent the rest of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:40.160
life involved in local politics and the defense of&nbsp;
the colony he became a rich man who owned several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:44.640
plantations and had over a hundred slaves he&nbsp;
also became a heavy drinker particularly fond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:49.200
of the local rum which he enjoyed while swapping&nbsp;
old war stories with other former privateers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:55.520
buccaneers he died august 25 1688 age 53 possibly&nbsp;
due to tuberculosis or possibly due to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.520 --> 00:19:00.400
drinking just four years later a giant earthquake&nbsp;
and tsunami hit port royal jamaica causing part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:05.040
the city to slip into the water quite fittingly&nbsp;
this included henry morgan's grave which became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:10.000
lost at sea forever so i really hope you found&nbsp;
